## 证明

**结论：是的。** 对于任意 $\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 以及任意自然数 $d$，总是存在 $\beta \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 使得 $\beta^d = \alpha$。

### 证明

回顾 $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 是 $\mathbb{Q}$ 的**代数闭包** (algebraic closure)。根据定义，代数闭包是一个代数闭域：$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 上的每一个非常数多项式在 $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 中都有一个根。

设 $\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 且 $d \in \mathbb{N}$。考虑多项式
$$f(x) = x^d - \alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}[x].$$

该多项式的系数属于 $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$（具体来说，首项系数为 $1 \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$，常数项为 $-\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$）。

**情况 $d \geq 1$：** 多项式 $f(x) = x^d - \alpha$ 是非常数多项式。由于 $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ 是## Proof

**Claim.** For any $\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and any natural number $d \geq 1$, there exists $\beta \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that $\beta^d = \alpha$.

**Proof.**

Recall that $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ denotes the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$, i.e., the field of all algebraic numbers. A standard theorem in algebra states:

> **$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is algebraically closed.** That is, every non-constant polynomial with coefficients in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has a root in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.

*Justification.* $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is by definition an algebraic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ that is algebraically closed (it is the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$). Equivalently, one can verify: if $f \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}[x]$ is non-constant, then $f$ has finitely many coefficients, each algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$, so they all lie in some finite extension $K/\mathbb{Q}$. Then $f \in K[x]$, and any root of $f$ is algebraic over $K$, hence algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$ (algebraicity is transitive), so the root lies in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. ∎

Now fix $\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a natural number $d \geq 1$. Consider the polynomial

$$f(x) = x^d - \alpha \;\in\; \overline{\mathbb{Q}}[x].$$

This is a non-constant polynomial (of degree $d \geq 1$) with coefficients in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Since $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is algebraically closed, $f$ has a root $\beta \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. By definition of a root,

$$\beta^d - \alpha = 0, \quad\text{i.e.,}\quad \beta^d = \alpha.$$

Therefore such a $\beta$ always exists. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
